{
  "program": "COUSR01C",
  "caseId": "case18",
  "description": "The same WHEN OTHER arm reached the other way, and the isolated proof of the eighty-into-seventy-eight narrowing. DFHPF12 is x'7C' and common.PfKeyResolver DOES resolve it, to PFK12 - but app/cbl/COUSR01C.cbl:90-103 names only DFHENTER, DFHPF3 and DFHPF4, so a resolvable key this program does not name still falls to :98-102. That is a different sub-branch from case17's unresolvable x'6B', and a resolver rewritten to return a default key instead of nothing would pass one and fail the other. The narrowing: WS-MESSAGE is declared PIC X(80) at :38 and ERRMSGO is PIC X(78) in app/cpy-bms/COUSR01.CPY, so MOVE WS-MESSAGE TO ERRMSGO at :188 drops the last two bytes on the right. expectedMessages states both channels at both widths, so the eighty and the seventy-eight are compared as different things: comparing across them would let the truncation pass unnoticed, which is exactly why the two channels exist. Every literal this program moves is far shorter than seventy-eight, so the two dropped bytes are always spaces - a property of the source, stated rather than assumed.",
